    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>

I am a student deeply engaged in mathematics, computer science, and linguistics in Japan, with interests in [effective altruism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effective_altruism) and [neoliberalism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neoliberalism).

At present, I am particularly interested in the following questions and areas of study.

**Mathematics.** My interests lie primarily in proof theory and algebra. In proof theory, I am studying axiomatic systems, model theory, and type theory. In algebra, I am especially interested in Cayley–Dickson algebras and hyperoperations, with a broader interest in the expansion of number systems and algebraic worlds.

**Computer Science.** I am interested in the relationship between computation and education. Currently, I am engaged in educational research at Kitakyushu City University, particularly concerning how computational tools and systems can support learning and knowledge transmission.

**Linguistics.** I am learning 20+ languages, including English, Spanish, Chinese, Korean, Arabic, Hindi, Persian, Russian, and German, with a broader interest in multilingual cognition and cross-linguistic structure.

This site serves as my digital garden—a quiet space for research notes, formal publications, and ongoing projects.

    </p>
